LAST-MODIFIED:20260821T180015Z
UID:10010125-1787335200-1787342400@unitedarts.org
SUMMARY:All American Exhibition Reception + HBCU Artist Residency Info Session
DESCRIPTION:Celebrate the work of 30 HBCU student and alumni artists and learn about Artspace's artist residency for HBCU alumni!\n\n\nJoin us for an evening celebrating the creativity\, legacy\, and continued impact of Historically Black Colleges and Universities through ALL AMERICAN\, a group exhibition featuring the work of more than thirty HBCU students and alumni from across the country. \nEnjoy music\, light refreshments\, and time to explore the exhibition while connecting with participating artists and the Artspace community. Throughout the evening\, guests are invited to visit current HBCU Alumni Artist-in-Residence Lamar Whidbee in the residency suite and attend an information session led by Artspace President and CEO Carly Jones and Artist Programs Manager Alexandra Jane to learn more about the HBCU Alumni Artist-in-Residence Program\, including its mission\, application process\, and residency experience. \nShow your school spirit by wearing your HBCU apparel\, Greek letters\, or representing your alma mater\, favorite team\, community organization\, or school colors! Visitors will also have the opportunity to have their “yearbook photo” taken by photographer Sanjé James\, in support of Artspace with a small suggested donation. \n \nEvent Schedule \n6:00–6:30 PM | Reception begins with music\, refreshments\, and exhibition viewing \n6:30–7:15 PM | HBCU Alumni Artist-in-Residence Information Session \n7:15–8:00 PM | Visit Lamar Whidbee’s residency studio\, enjoy the exhibition\, connect with artists\, and have your yearbook photo taken \n \nAbout the HBCU Alumni Artist Residency \nThis year-long residency focuses on graduates from historically Black colleges and universities (HBCUs). Upon graduation\, students often lose resources\, equipment\, colleagues\, and mentors. The HBCU Alumni Residency seeks to remedy those issues by extending the learning process and broadening the experience for graduates to build their careers as visual artists. There are more 4-year HBCUs in North Carolina than anywhere else in the country\, and this residency aims to highlight and support the many talented artists graduating from these culturally rich institutions. This career-stimulating residency for an emerging artist includes a rent-free studio for one year with 24-hour access within a collaborative environment with 30+ other professional artists and art administrators.  \n \nAbout Artspace \nArtspace is a non-profit visual arts center located in Downtown Raleigh. Visit Artspace to explore exhibitions featuring national and community artists\, engage with more than 30 artists working in their studios\, and enjoy art classes and community events for all ages and abilities. To learn more about Artspace\, visit artspacenc.org \n \nParking and Accessibility \nFree street parking is available on Davie Street. Free parking is available at the following parking decks: \n\nCity Center Deck\, 429 S. Wilmington St.\nMoore Square Deck\, 233 S. Wilmington St.\nMunicipal Complex Deck\, 201 W. Morgan St.\nWilmington Street Station Deck\, 117 S. Wilmington St.\n\nThere are also several pay lots nearby: City Market parking lot and several Secure P lots for night + weekend parking (314 E Davie St\, 415 S Person St\, 417 S Person St). \nExplore accessibility information with our Know Before You Go Guide. \n \nFeatured images (left to right): Garvey 1914 by Jessica Kelly; What’s Central To You\, Not Us by Derrick Davis; Resilience in Bloom by Karrington Gardner
URL:https://unitedarts.org/calendar/all-american-exhibition-reception-hbcu-artist-residency-info-session/
LOCATION:Artspace\, 201 East Davie Street\, Raleigh\, NC\, 27601\, United States
ATTACH;FMTTYPE=image/jpeg:https://unitedarts.org/wp-content/uploads/2026/08/823a52f95e8189e27bbdc658258d91b5.jpg
END:VEVENT
